---
import FadeIn from "./FadeIn.tsx";
import RiskCard from "./RiskCard.astro";
---

<div>
  <FadeIn client:visible>
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-center">
      Vibe coding gets you a demo.<br class="hidden md:block" /> It doesn't get
      you a product.
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-neutral-400 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed text-center">
      AI tools can generate a working app in minutes, but "working" and
      "ready for real users" are very different things.
    </p>
  </FadeIn>

  <FadeIn client:visible>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 md:gap-8 mt-12">
      <RiskCard
        number="01 / Security"
        title="Your users' data is exposed and you don't know it"
        pullquote="45% of AI-generated code contains an OWASP Top 10 vulnerability."
        pullquoteSource="Veracode, 2025"
      >
        <svg
          slot="icon"
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
          width="20"
          height="20"
          viewBox="0 0 24 24"
          fill="none"
          stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="2"
          stroke-linecap="round"
          stroke-linejoin="round"
          ><path
            d="M20 13c0 5-3.5 7.5-7.66 8.95a1 1 0 0 1-.67-.01C7.5 20.5 4 18 4 13V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1c2 0 4.5-1.2 6.24-2.72a1.17 1.17 0 0 1 1.52 0C14.51 3.81 17 5 19 5a1 1 0 0 1 1 1z"
          ></path><path d="M12 8v4"></path><path d="M12 16h.01"></path></svg
        >
        <p>
          In July 2025, the Tea dating app leaked 72,000 private images and
          13,000 government IDs because its AI-generated Firebase code had
          zero authentication. Researchers found similar patterns across
          5,600+ vibe-coded apps. 2,000 vulnerabilities, 400+ exposed
          secrets, and leaked medical records and bank details.
        </p>
      </RiskCard>

      <RiskCard
        number="02 / Control"
        title="One AI command wiped 2.5 years of production data"
        pullquote="I over-relied on the AI agent to run Terraform commands. I treated plan, apply, and destroy as something that could be delegated. That removed the last safety layer."
        pullquoteSource="Alexey Grigorev, DataTalks.Club founder"
      >
        <svg
          slot="icon"
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
          width="20"
          height="20"
          viewBox="0 0 24 24"
          fill="none"
          stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="2"
          stroke-linecap="round"
          stroke-linejoin="round"
          ><polyline points="4 17 10 11 4 5"></polyline><line
            x1="12"
            y1="19"
            x2="20"
            y2="19"></line></svg
        >
        <p>
          A developer delegated an AWS migration to an AI coding agent. After
          a forgotten state file left Terraform blind to existing
          infrastructure, the agent ran <code class="text-xs bg-[var(--color-surface-2)] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">terraform destroy</code>
          on production, wiping the VPC, database, load balancers, and 1.9
          million rows of course data in seconds.
        </p>
      </RiskCard>

      <RiskCard
        number="03 / Scalability"
        title="It breaks the moment it succeeds"
        pullquote="It worked for a small dataset, but as soon as real-world traffic hit, the system slowed to a crawl."
        pullquoteSource="CTO quoted in Addy Osmani's analysis of vibe coding failures"
      >
        <svg
          slot="icon"
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
          width="20"
          height="20"
          viewBox="0 0 24 24"
          fill="none"
          stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="2"
          stroke-linecap="round"
          stroke-linejoin="round"
          ><polyline points="22 17 13.5 8.5 8.5 13.5 2 7"></polyline><polyline
            points="16 17 22 17 22 11"></polyline></svg
        >
        <p>
          One startup's Vercel bill jumped from $20/month to $700+ after a
          single traffic spike. AI-generated code wasn't optimized for
          serverless costs. Another founder's AI coding assistant deleted
          their entire production database during a "cleanup," overriding a
          direct instruction not to modify anything.
        </p>
      </RiskCard>

      <RiskCard
        number="04 / Technical Debt"
        title="Your first hire will want to rewrite everything"
        pullquote="Vibe coding creates an illusion of success until the system begins to wobble under workloads."
        pullquoteSource="Engineering leader quoted in Medium"
      >
        <svg
          slot="icon"
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
          width="20"
          height="20"
          viewBox="0 0 24 24"
          fill="none"
          stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="2"
          stroke-linecap="round"
          stroke-linejoin="round"
          ><path d="M15 7h2a5 5 0 0 1 0 10h-2m-6 0H7A5 5 0 0 1 7 7h2"
          ></path></svg
        >
        <p>
          You raise a round, hire your first engineer, and they open the
          codebase to find:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Business logic scattered across components</li>
          <li>Hardcoded values everywhere</li>
          <li>No tests, no docs, no structure</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          That engineer will need 3 months to rewrite before shipping a
          single feature. Your runway just got shorter.
        </p>
      </RiskCard>
    </div>
  </FadeIn>
</div>
